{"name":"Swamp Fox","hex":"#b79d69","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"swamp","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An area of wet (water-saturated), spongy (soft) land, often with trees, generally a rich ecosystem for certain plants and animals but ill-suited for many agricultural purposes.","A place or situation that is foul or where progress is difficult."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To drench or fill with water.","To overwhelm; to make too busy, or overrun the capacity of."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/swamp"},{"term":"fox","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-5 language code for Formosan languages."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small-to-medium-sized canine mammal, related to dogs and wolves, with a flattened skull, upright triangular ears, a pointed, slightly upturned snout, and a long bushy tail, of the following groups.","Other canines that resemble true foxes, of the genera Cerdocyon, Lycalopex, Otocyon, and Urocyon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fox"}],"entries":[{"title":"Francis Marion","blocks":[{"text":"Brigadier General Francis Marion, also known as the \"Swamp Fox\", was an American military officer, planter, and politician who served during the French and Indian War and the Revolutionary War. During the American Revolution, Marion supported the Patriot cause and enlisted in the Continental Army, fighting against British forces in the southern theater of the American Revolutionary War from 1780 to 1781."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/General_Francis_Marion_%28NYPL_b13050113-421073%29.tiff/lossy-page1-330px-General_Francis_Marion_%28NYPL_b13050113-421073%29.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Marion","wikidata":"Q960315"},{"title":"Swamp Fox (roller coaster)","blocks":[{"text":"The Swamp Fox is a wooden roller coaster located in Myrtle Beach, South Carolina, United States. It is one of 37 rides at Family Kingdom Amusement Park. The coaster is named for American Revolutionary War leader Francis Marion, who was nicknamed the \"Swamp Fox\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Swamp_Fox_at_Family_Kingdom%2C_Myrtle_Beach%2C_SC.jpg/330px-Swamp_Fox_at_Family_Kingdom%2C_Myrtle_Beach%2C_SC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swamp_Fox_(roller_coaster)","wikidata":"Q7653328"},{"title":"Swamp","blocks":[{"text":"A swamp is a forested wetland. Swamps are considered to be transition zones because both land and water play a role in creating this environment. Swamps vary in size and are located all around the world. The water of a swamp may be fresh water, brackish water, or seawater. Freshwater swamps form along large rivers or lakes where they are critically dependent upon rainwater and seasonal flooding to maintain natural water level fluctuations. Saltwater swamps are found along tropical and subtropical coastlines. Some swamps have hammocks, or dry-land protrusions, covered by aquatic vegetation, or vegetation that tolerates periodic inundation or soil saturation. The two main types of swamp are \"true\" or swamp forests and \"transitional\" or shrub swamps. In the boreal regions of Canada, the word swamp is colloquially used for what is more formally termed a bog, fen, or muskeg. Some of the world's largest swamps are found along major rivers such as the Amazon, the Mississippi, and the Congo."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Florida_freshwater_swamp_usgov_image.jpg/330px-Florida_freshwater_swamp_usgov_image.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swamp","wikidata":"Q166735"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/General_Francis_Marion_%28NYPL_b13050113-421073%29.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-General_Francis_Marion_%28NYPL_b13050113-421073%29.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/General_Francis_Marion_%28NYPL_b13050113-421073%29.tiff/lossy-page1-1280px-General_Francis_Marion_%28NYPL_b13050113-421073%29.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/General_Francis_Marion_%28NYPL_b13050113-421073%29.tiff/lossy-page1-500px-General_Francis_Marion_%28NYPL_b13050113-421073%29.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeneral_Francis_Marion_(NYPL_b13050113-421073).tiff"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/General_Marion.jpg/500px-General_Marion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/General_Marion.jpg/500px-General_Marion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" General Marion Inviting a British Officer to Share His Meal by John Blake White; his slave Oscar Marion kneels at the left of the group.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeneral_Marion.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Banastre-Tarleton-by-Joshua-Reynolds.jpg/500px-Banastre-Tarleton-by-Joshua-Reynolds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Banastre-Tarleton-by-Joshua-Reynolds.jpg/1280px-Banastre-Tarleton-by-Joshua-Reynolds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Banastre-Tarleton-by-Joshua-Reynolds.jpg/500px-Banastre-Tarleton-by-Joshua-Reynolds.jpg?utm_sou